[ROOT CAUSE — CONFIRMED]

## The mechanism

Cascade failure dynamics in the Kubernetes control plane running on
resource-constrained hardware.

Sequence of events during a cascade:
1. Trigger event: any minor perturbation — OOM kill of single pod,
   brief network hiccup, etcd slow-apply, probe timeout, anything
2. One or more components fail liveness probe
3. Kubelet restarts the failed container
4. Restart generates IO load:
   - Container image mount
   - Log file rotation/creation
   - Controller does full LIST against apiserver
   - Apiserver queries etcd
5. This IO load pushes other nearby components past their own
   probe timeouts
6. Those components also restart
7. Now multiple components are restarting simultaneously, competing
   for the same NVMe bandwidth
8. Each restart attempt is slower than it would be in isolation
9. Slower starts mean MORE probes failing
10. Feedback loop: failures cause load causes more failures
11. Without intervention, this self-sustains indefinitely

## Why it worked for weeks then failed

The cluster has a normal operating envelope where baseline load
fits within NVMe capacity. Day-to-day, minor perturbations happen
but are absorbed without cascade because the system has enough
headroom.

What pushed it over: master1 hung for hours this morning. During
that time, the cluster ran in degraded 2-master mode. etcd was
accepting writes, kubelet was reporting, but there was no master1
to participate in quorum or serve its share of apiserver traffic.

When master1 eventually rebooted:
- Had to resync etcd delta (hours of writes to catch up)
- Apiserver came back and immediately faced full scrape/LIST load
- Probes fired before components were stable
- Cascade began
- Has been in various stages of cascade since 01:41 local

Each subsequent action we took (reboots, drains, shutdowns) either
triggered a new cascade or extended the existing one.

The EXPERIMENT that confirmed this:
- Scaling all workloads to 0 → instant stability
- No hardware change, no memory change, nothing else different
- Just: remove the load that was driving the cascade
- Therefore: load on the hardware was the issue, not the hardware itself

## Why it's NOT the things we initially suspected

Hardware: Same hardware ran stable for weeks. Only fails when
cascade is running.

Memory (primary cause): VMs were running with same memory for weeks.
Bumping master memory probably helps resilience but wasn't the
actual cause of tonight's event.

etcd DB: 46 MB, tiny, healthy. Ruled out.

TS-K8S-038 qemu-ga bug: qemu-ga was consistently at 0% CPU throughout.
No strace evidence of EAGAIN loop. Ruled out for these occurrences.
(Original doc's occurrences 1-3 had captured strace evidence and
are legitimate TS-K8S-038 cases. Different failure mode.)

SSSD/NSS: User confirmed earlier that SSSD restart at 02:51 was
normal VM boot, not mid-operation restart. Ruled out.

Lid switch/logind: Logind bursts are downstream symptom of host
being IO-blocked, not cause. Ruled out.

Prometheus scrape storms: Prometheus was running continuously for
14 hours before first spike. Not a trigger.

NFS backend: Was not specifically tested but cluster recovered fully
without touching NFS, which it would not have if NFS was the problem.

