[Gotchas Encountered]

## etcd env variable conflict
etcd v3.6+ is strict about env var + flag conflicts.
If ETCDCTL_ENDPOINTS is set in environment AND --endpoints is used
as flag, the command fails with fatal error.

Fix: either unset the env vars, or use them exclusively (no flags).
Also ETCDCTL_API=3 is now unrecognized in v3.6 — emits warning but
otherwise harmless (v3 is the only API now).

## Drain-before-shutdown order
Proper order: cordon → drain → delete node → shutdown VM
Alternative used tonight: cordon → drain → shutdown VM → delete node
Both work, but the "proper" order leaves cleaner state.

## Cordon vs drain vs delete
- Cordon = "no new pods here" (existing pods stay)
- Drain = "evict existing pods"
- Delete node = "remove node object from API"

User initially asked why cordoned node wasn't removed — needed to
explain the 3-step process.

## worker3 came back automatically
Remediation controller brought worker3 back up without being asked.
This was noted but not stopped because it was harmless.
Indicates remediation is doing its job, but also means you can't
just "turn things off" without considering automation.

## Flux will revert scaling
If Flux controllers are running while you scale workloads down,
Flux reconciliation will scale them back up to match git.
Must suspend kustomizations or commit reduced replicas to git BEFORE
scaling down, OR start flux LAST after reducing workloads.

## memory inconsistency after worker3 rejoined
worker3 came back at 2.75 GB while workers 1 and 2 are at 3 GB.
Our memory change didn't apply to worker3 because it wasn't in the
cluster when we did the change.

ACTION ITEM: Reconcile worker3 memory to match or standardize all
workers. Not urgent since cluster is stable.

## "why did it work for weeks then fail today?"
This question from the user was THE turning point of the investigation.
Every theory before this treated the cluster as "always broken" but
that framing was wrong — it was recently broken.

Should have been asked earlier. Lesson: when investigating anomalies,
first establish the baseline of normal operation and ask what changed.

